there was a stage production of a play  by Peter quilter called end of the  rainbow produced in 2005 I believe what  are your recollections of seeing it and  what struck you about it and made you  think this is a film well I just thought  I didn't know anything about this story  I just thought what a remarkable story  and thought well I'll option it and then  the next day it was on the front cover  of Arts and Leisure New York Times I  think LA Times LA Times and I thought  well that's that then somebody else has  got it Scott Rudin's got it but I knew  the producer of the play and I thought  well I'll hang on in there and we got it  and it was just it was just you know  there was something remarkable about it  that you could have this huge star  having been in London for this period of  time and having died in London and I  thought well we should do something with  this so Rosalyn Wilder I tracked down  the real Rosalyn Wilder who used in the  film and she opened the gates for a she  introduced us to people have never  spoken about Judy Garland ever so a  dresser who lived in South Africa who  you know very tentatively spoke to me  and said I haven't spoken about Judy for  40 years we spoke to people that were  members of the band we spoke to dancers  and so we gradually kind of this story  emerge that was much more detailed and a  kind of the three hander version of the  play that I hope has made it more you  know real and genuine and giving it a  fabric that it didn't have before but we  were you know he was thrilling Rupert  this film I really feel like allowed you  to combine you know your different  talents of theatre and film and bring a  lot of your theater type experience to  bear for the film was that part of the  appeal for you yeah I guess maybe it's  somewhat of a life thing I felt that I'd  spent 20 years in rehearsal rooms very  close to extraordinary performers and  singers and there's something about  being in a room where you're as close as  we are now which is you never get on the  stage because there's a natural distance  and I felt like kind of witnessing the  extraordinary pressures the performers  go through this sort of transcendent  glory that they can find kind of in the  discovery of their talent particularly  around music and singing and opera and  musical theater that kind of thing I  felt like it would be wonderful if we  could try and obviously garland you know  had that in spades if we could try and  capture what it meant to perform and and  and and sing and the costs of that and  also the the calling of that anyway as  well aside from all the extraordinary  details of her life Renee you've  obviously sung in a film before Chicago  and got an Oscar nomination for it but  when approached with this yes when  approached with this idea when you first  heard about the idea of playing Judy if  one side of the spectrum is I'm in and  the other side of the spectrum is nope  too scary where did you fall at the  beginning the thoughts going between the  two kept me up at night for quite some  time and then there was this shift in  perspective there was no conversation  about the official beginning of the  process there was just an invitation to  come and have a little chat  and so the fellows invited me to London  to just talk about it we'll just talk  about why you know the motivation for  making the film why to tell this story  at this you know about this chapter in  her life try some makeup looks let's try  this wig and then they said and we'll  try some songs and we'll book some time  at Abbey Road and you'll just come and  you'll practice some songs at Abbey Road  and I said let me just put that in my  calendar I'll be right there one thing I  didn't know until yesterday morning was  that you did part of your process and in  learning the voice and getting  comfortable with the voice in your car  often times on the 405 freeway so if if  one of us were to have been driving past  you on the 405 at the exact moment that  you were figuring this character out  what would we have seen  you would have probably called EMS  thinking that the lady next to you is  having a seizure I'm sure of it there  was a lot of contortion there was a lot  of contorting the torso to get the sound  out initially it yeah that or else you  might have just thought wow she's she's  really having fun in there isn't she  David when you think back to putting  this whole production together what was  the biggest challenge as far as finding  the perfect location for something the  one the one that was most important was  the talk of the town so it was the  Hackney Empire that we chose in the end  and we went to see near derelict  theatres in Stratham in with roofs  falling in and Roop it was going yeah we  can rebuild it but what was important  was to find and this is sort of slightly  crossover to you as well but we needed  to find a summer with a thrust stage you  know with which was what the talk of the  town had and it had this black inky  stage and we needed that to be to be  part of it and again coming back to  Rosalind Wilder Rosslyn was the one that  kind of gave us all these pictures from  way back when I had all these snapshots  so she was quite useful in that regard  so we knew what the fine feathers who  the dancers knew exactly what they  looked like and how they dressed and we  knew exactly what the stage would look  like I don't know if you want to say  anything on yeah that particular thrust  stage architectural is certainly in  London is quite hard to find because  it's about the relationship between the  circle and the stage and most proscenium  Victorian theatres have a closer circle  so you couldn't build out a thrust stage  the Hackney Empire which is restored by  Big Lottery grant about 15 years ago is  a lyric house it's four operator and I  guess opera performance is in the late  19th century and it allowed us to build  out further but even then there was a  lot of logistical problems about finding  it that it was gonna be free for the  amount of time we had we had this very  narrow window six days to I think with  three days to fit up and then six days  to shoot and get out and it was pretty  crazy I know it's important to you that  people understand that this was yes it's  your performance but there were a lot of  people at play to help you create what  we saw who were the people who you  remember as being the most integral  well these guys were there for the whole  thing and and it did it felt like a  fluid sort of a series of experiments we  just started trying things with that  visit in London and then we just  continued on from there we worked with  Jeremy Woodhouse and Rob crafter who was  his assistant who did fantastic makeup I  mean just fantastic every day you know  the the glam was period and the  prosthetics were I think pretty hard to  find hard to see and it was just a big  you know a big process and they just a  great job trying everything we just  tried we first put it all on and then  took it all off and I would have kept it  all on if I could have more to hide  behind but and then we you know the  composer Matt Dinkley was there working  when the music every day and and then  Andrew the piano player warming me up  and he was like cheering yes you can and  let's see who else was I mean there's  some journey to meme who made these  beautiful costumes which are just out of  this world  I mean inspired by Judy's dress in the  days and she used antique sequins from  France from the period to make these  dresses as authentically as she could  and and then she fit them to my assumed  posture so that they wouldn't zip up if  I wasn't standing like I was opposed to  yes so I mean all of these you know is  true to be disciplined by antique  sequins and just this way just to throw  in since we've done the list Cave Quinn  who did the production design and just  you know who did that an amazing job and  like you know we're working it's not a  50 million dollar budget you know so  she's you know she's trying to  accommodate everything that Rupert's  constantly demanding every day and doing  an amazing job I mean it's just it looks  fantastic  so Renee when you think about everything  that went into perfecting Judy's  performance  there's the singing there's the dancing  and the movement there and there's the  unique facial expressions which you  capture so beautifully when you think  about all those three things  was there one aspect of it that was  particularly  to feel like you had it mastered about  perfecting and mastering any night I'm  feeling good about it I feel like gosh  it's so interesting when I think back on  this I don't think about it in the  traditional sense of making a film  because it didn't feel like the same  kind of process again it felt like we  were trying things together you know and  we were all digging around to see if  like Rupert called it mining for  treasure we were mining for these little  bits that seemed essential to collect in  order to conjure her essence in some way  and it she's felt like she was  everywhere her photographs and the sound  of her voice and interviews and her  music and and you know the stories going  around and the things that somebody had  discovered the night before that they  bring that day so it felt like I don't  know it was it was almost palpable in  every room that we were working in this  essence of this extraordinary person and  the legacy of her work was very much  alive it sort of just started just to  soak in for everybody and in terms of  difficulty it was such a joy that I  can't look at it like that it just felt  like spoiled rotten you know what a  wonderful professional obligation to  have you know to go down the Judy rabbit  hole and just you know feast on that  every day but some things took more time  but we had time so in the beginning that  the year before we had that time so it's  great to be here with all of you thanks  to all of you for sticking around

